Presidential elections were held in [[Cyprus]] on 28 January 2018.<ref name=IC1>[http://in-cyprus.com/lillikas-is-first-official-2018-cyprus-presidential-election-runner/ Lillikas becomes first official 2018 presidential candidate] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170827213817/http://in-cyprus.com/lillikas-is-first-official-2018-cyprus-presidential-election-runner/ |date=27 August 2017 }} In Cyprus, 13 March 2017</ref> As no candidate received a majority of the vote in the first round, a run-off was held on 4 February between the top two candidates, incumbent President [[Nicos Anastasiades]] of the [[Democratic Rally]] (DISY) and [[Stavros Malas]] of the [[Progressive Party of Working People]]. Anastasiades emerged as the winner with 55.99% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://cyprus-mail.com/2018/02/05/second-term-anastasiades-says-still-left/|title=Second term for Anastasiades, says more still left to do|publisher=[[Cyprus Mail]]|first=Jean|last=Christou|date=5 February 2018|access-date=5 February 2018|archive-date=5 February 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180205113818/http://cyprus-mail.com/2018/02/05/second-term-anastasiades-says-still-left/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The [[President of Cyprus]] is elected using the [[two-round system]];<ref>[http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2267/ Cyprus] IFES</ref> if no candidate gets a majority in the first round of voting, a run-off is held between the top two candidates.

